[Thinking]
**Exploring string conversions**

The user seems to be working with C++ and wants to know how to convert `std::string` to `std::vector<char>` or `std::vector<std::string>`. For a `vector<char>`, I can create it using `std::vector<char> v(s.begin(), s.end());`, and to include a null terminator, I could use `v(s.c_str(), s.c_str() + s.size() + 1)`. If the user needs an array of ASCII codes, I suggest `vector<int> v(s.begin(), s.end());`, but it requires type casting. I'll definitely include code snippets to illustrate these conversions!
[/Thinking]

There are a few common meanings of "convert std::string to std::vector" — the usual one is turning a string into a vector of its characters. Here are concise examples for the common cases.

1) std::vector<char> from std::string (no null terminator)
```cpp
std::string s = "hello";
std::vector<char> v(s.begin(), s.end());  // {'h','e','l','l','o'}
```

2) include the terminating NUL
```cpp
std::vector<char> v_with_null(s.c_str(), s.c_str() + s.size() + 1); // includes '\0'
```

3) std::vector<unsigned char> (bytes)
```cpp
std::vector<unsigned char> bytes(s.begin(), s.end());
// or explicit conversion if you prefer:
std::vector<unsigned char> bytes2;
bytes2.reserve(s.size());
for (unsigned char c : s) bytes2.push_back(c); // or: bytes2.push_back(static_cast<unsigned char>(c));
```

4) std::vector<int> of code points (ASCII codes)
```cpp
std::vector<int> codes;
codes.reserve(s.size());
for (unsigned char c : s) codes.push_back(static_cast<int>(c));
```

5) std::vector<std::string> when you want the whole string as a single element
```cpp
std::vector<std::string> vs = { s }; // one-element vector
```

6) split string into tokens (e.g., whitespace)
```cpp
std::istringstream iss(s);
std::vector<std::string> tokens;
std::string token;
while (iss >> token) tokens.push_back(token);
```

Choose the variant that matches what you need (characters, bytes, numeric codes, tokens, or a single-element vector).

